WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.382
A practical cleaning workflow can be organized into six steps.

00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:10.497
First, profile the data. Measure the problems before changing anything.

00:00:10.497 --> 00:00:11.802
Second, preserve the source.

00:00:11.802 --> 00:00:19.957
Keep the raw evidence available so you can always compare a cleaned result with its original value.

00:00:19.957 --> 00:00:24.677
Third, normalize representations that have a known meaning.

00:00:24.677 --> 00:00:29.627
For example, trimming extra spaces is usually a safe normalization.

00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:34.242
Fourth, validate values against documented rules.

00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:43.168
Fifth, resolve problems that require a decision, such as duplicate records or rows that should be quarantined for

00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:47.857
review. And sixth, prevent the same problems from appearing again.

00:00:47.857 --> 00:00:55.898
Once a rule is stable and well understood, we may enforce it with constraints or validation during data entry.

00:00:55.898 --> 00:01:02.008
Notice the order. Prevention comes after we understand the data and the rules.

00:01:02.008 --> 00:01:07.666
Good cleaning is a cycle of measuring, deciding, checking, and improving.
